%requires qore >= 3.0

%modern

%requires WebSocketHandler >= 2.0

# HttpServerUtil for the WebSocket handler's request handling
%requires HttpServerUtil

# Linenoise support for line editing and history
%requires linenoise

# JSON support for WebSocket message protocol
%requires json

# Reflection support for introspection commands
%requires reflection

# Util module for TerminalColor
%requires Util

# QoreCodeFormat module for auto-indentation support
%requires QoreCodeFormat

# QoreHistory module for shared history management
%requires(reexport) QoreHistory

# QoreCodeCompletion module for shared code completion engine (re-exported for CompletionItem)
%requires(reexport) QoreCodeCompletion

module QoreRepl {
    version = "1.0";
    desc = "Qore REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop) module";
    author = "Qore Technologies, s.r.o.";
    url = "https://qoretechnologies.com";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage QoreRepl Module

    @section qorereplintro Introduction to the QoreRepl Module

    The %QoreRepl module provides a reusable REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop) implementation
    for Qore. It can be used from:
    - Command-line applications (with linenoise for line editing)
    - Network handlers (WebSocket, HTTP, etc.)
    - WebSocket server for remote REPL access

    The module provides:
    - @ref QoreRepl::QoreRepl "QoreRepl": Core REPL class for evaluation
    - @ref QoreRepl::QoreReplActionSession "QoreReplActionSession": Action-based REPL session
    - @ref QoreRepl::QoreReplWebSocketHandler "QoreReplWebSocketHandler": WebSocket handler for HttpServer
    - @ref QoreRepl::QoreReplWebSocketConnection "QoreReplWebSocketConnection": Per-client WebSocket connection

    The core @ref QoreRepl::QoreRepl "QoreRepl" class handles:
    - Incremental parsing using Qore's parsePending/parseCommit API
    - Multi-line input detection (incomplete statements)
    - Result formatting
    - Special commands (/help, /vars, /reset, etc.)

    @section qorerepl_usage Basic Usage

    @subsection qorerepl_basic Basic Evaluation

    @code{.py}
%requires QoreRepl

QoreRepl repl();

# Simple expression
*hash<auto> result = repl.eval("1 + 1");
if (result.value) {
    printf("Result: %s\n", QoreRepl::formatValue(result.value));
}

# Multi-line input
result = repl.eval("if (True) {");  # Returns NOTHING - needs more input
printf("Prompt: %s\n", repl.getPrompt());  # "    > "
result = repl.eval("    print('hello');");
result = repl.eval("}");  # Now complete, executes
    @endcode

    At the continuation prompt, every submitted line is literal Qore source. Slash-prefixed lines and
    history shortcuts such as @c !! are not interpreted as REPL commands, including inside multiline strings.
    Finish the source or cancel it before using commands at the primary @c qore> prompt. Linenoise uses Ctrl+C;
    embedders call @ref QoreRepl::QoreRepl::reset() "reset()" to clear pending source and indentation without
    discarding the Program. Command completion and @c inspectRequest() follow the same input mode.

    @code{.py}
repl.eval("if (True) {");
repl.reset();  # Cancel this unfinished block; evaluated variables remain available.
repl.eval("/threads");  # Commands are available again at the primary prompt.
    @endcode

    @subsection qorerepl_save Saving and Loading Code

    Use @c /save followed by a filename to export the session's entered Qore code.
    Missing statement terminators are added using the same rules as evaluation;
    existing terminators and block definitions are preserved. REPL commands such
    as @c /vars and @c /save are omitted. The interactive history stays unchanged.
    Load the exported file with @c /load followed by its filename in a fresh session.

    @code{.py}
QoreRepl original({"history_file": ""});
original.eval("our int answer = 40 + 2");
original.eval("/save session.q");
QoreRepl restored({"history_file": ""});
restored.eval("/load session.q");
*hash<auto> result = restored.eval("answer");  # result.value is 42
    @endcode

    Saving exports entered code, including unsuccessful evaluations; it is not a
    serialized program-state snapshot. Loading executes that code again, so external
    side effects and invalid input retain their ordinary execution semantics.

    @subsection qorerepl_threads Background Threads and Parsing

    Parsing remains serialized: Qore cannot reparse a REPL Program while its code is active. Code submissions,
    @c /type, @c /load and @c /reset return @c REPL-THREADS-ACTIVE before changing pending input or history when
    threads are running. Use @c /threads to inspect Program thread IDs and names, @c /cancel followed by an ID or
    @c all to request cooperative termination, and @c /join followed by an ID or @c all to wait for Program
    activity to finish. These commands run outside the evaluated Program and therefore remain available while
    parsing is blocked. Cancellation can be caught or deferred; it never forcibly kills a native thread.

    Embedders use @ref QoreRepl::QoreRepl::inspectRequest() "inspectRequest()" to offer Wait, Cancel request, and
    Terminate threads before submission. Capture @ref QoreRepl::QoreRepl::getProgram() "getProgram()" and call
    @ref Qore::Program::waitForThreads() "waitForThreads()" from a worker, then post its completion to the UI's
    event loop. This condition-driven wait includes starting threads and descendants. Cancel the monitor worker
    when the UI closes; cancelling a pending request leaves the Program and input intact. Recheck before retrying:
    an idle notification does not reserve the Program against new externally-started work.

    @code{.py}
QoreRepl repl();
repl.eval("background sub () { sleep(40); }()");
repl.eval("/threads");
# eval("/reset") now returns REPL-THREADS-ACTIVE promptly.
repl.eval("/cancel all");
repl.eval("/join all");
repl.eval("/reset");
    @endcode

    @c /join waits for threads to leave this Program, including its exit handlers, not for final process-level
    thread-local cleanup. @ref Qore::thread_join() "thread_join()" provides that stronger guarantee to an embedder
    that owns the process-level thread. @ref QoreRepl::QoreRepl::getHelpText() "getHelpText(False)" omits linenoise
    shortcuts so other frontends can supply their own keyboard help.

    @subsection qorerepl_websocket WebSocket Server Example

    The module includes built-in WebSocket server support. Simply add the handler to an HttpServer:

    @code{.py}
%requires HttpServer
%requires QoreRepl

# Create REPL WebSocket handler
QoreReplWebSocketHandler handler();

# Optionally add authentication
handler.setAuthCallback(sub (hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr) returns bool {
    return hdr."x-api-key" == "secret123";
});

# Add to HTTP server
HttpServer server({"port": 8080});
server.addHandler("/ws/repl", handler);
server.start();

# Clients connect to ws://localhost:8080/ws/repl
    @endcode

    @subsection qorerepl_protocol WebSocket Message Protocol

    The WebSocket handler uses a JSON message protocol. See the AsyncAPI schema
    at @c QoreRepl/QoreReplApi.asyncapi.yaml for complete message definitions.

    Client to Server:
    - @c {"type": "eval", "code": "1 + 1"} - Evaluate code
    - @c {"type": "command", "command": "/help"} - Execute REPL command
    - @c {"type": "complete", "input": "pri"} - Request code completions
    - @c {"type": "ping"} - Keepalive

    Server to Client:
    - @c {"type": "result", "value": "2"} - Evaluation result
    - @c {"type": "error", "error": "PARSE-ERROR", "description": "..."} - Error
    - @c {"type": "prompt", "value": "qore> ", "needsMoreInput": false} - Prompt update
    - @c {"type": "completion", "id": "...", "items": [...]} - Completion results
    - @c {"type": "session", "sessionId": "...", "version": "1.0"} - Session info on connect

    @section qorerepl_sandboxing Sandboxing and Security

    The QoreRepl class supports sandboxing through Qore's parse options. You can restrict
    what code the REPL can execute by passing parse options when creating the underlying
    Program object.

    @subsection qorerepl_sandbox_usage Sandboxed REPL Example

    @code{.py}
%requires QoreRepl

# Create a sandboxed REPL that cannot access filesystem, network, or external processes
int sandbox_options = PO_NO_FILESYSTEM | PO_NO_NETWORK | PO_NO_EXTERNAL_PROCESS;
QoreRepl repl({"parse_options": sandbox_options});

# Filesystem commands will show graceful error messages
*hash<auto> result = repl.eval("/pwd");
# Result: {"error": {"err": "SANDBOX-ERROR", "desc": "filesystem access is restricted..."}}

# Code that tries to access restricted resources will fail
result = repl.eval("File::readTextFile('/etc/passwd')");
# Result: {"error": {"err": "PARSE-EXCEPTION", "desc": "..."}}
    @endcode

    @subsection qorerepl_sandbox_options Available Parse Options for Sandboxing

    Common parse options for sandboxing:

    |!Parse Option|!Description
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM|Disables filesystem access (affects /pwd, /cd, /load, /save commands)
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_NETWORK|Disables network access (affects Socket, HTTPClient, etc.)
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_PROCESS|Disables external process execution (system(), backquote, etc.)
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL|Disables process control functions (exit(), fork(), etc.)
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL|Disables thread control (background operator, thread_exit)
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_DATABASE|Disables database access

    @subsection qorerepl_sandbox_commands Command Behavior in Sandboxed Mode

    When filesystem access is restricted (@ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM), the following
    REPL commands will display helpful error messages instead of failing silently:

    - @c /pwd - Shows "filesystem access is restricted (PO_NO_FILESYSTEM)"
    - @c /cd - Shows "filesystem access is restricted (PO_NO_FILESYSTEM)"
    - @c /load - Shows "filesystem access is restricted (PO_NO_FILESYSTEM)"
    - @c /save - Shows "filesystem access is restricted (PO_NO_FILESYSTEM)"

    All other REPL commands continue to work normally in sandboxed mode, including
    introspection commands (/vars, /functions, /classes, etc.) and session commands
    (/help, /reset, /history, etc.).

    Code completion offers only the classes and functions that the parse options allow, by the same rule as the
    parser: for example, @ref Qore::File "File" is not offered with @ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM, and
    @ref Qore::DebugProgram "DebugProgram", in a functional domain that a Program has to allow explicitly, is offered
    only with @ref Qore::PO_ALLOW_DEBUGGER.

    @subsection qorerepl_websocket_sandbox Sandboxed WebSocket Server

    You can also create sandboxed WebSocket REPL servers:

    @code{.py}
%requires HttpServer
%requires QoreRepl

# Create a sandboxed WebSocket REPL handler
int sandbox_options = PO_NO_FILESYSTEM | PO_NO_NETWORK | PO_NO_EXTERNAL_PROCESS;
QoreReplWebSocketHandler handler({"parse_options": sandbox_options});

# Add authentication for additional security
handler.setAuthCallback(sub (hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr) returns bool {
    return hdr."x-api-key" == "secret123";
});

# Add to HTTP server
HttpServer server({"port": 8080});
server.addHandler("/ws/repl", handler);
server.start();
    @endcode

    @section qorerepl_relnotes Release Notes

    @subsection qorerepl_v1_0 v1.0
    - initial release
*/

#! Main namespace for the QoreRepl module
public namespace QoreRepl {
}
